## Tiers (#2796)

Alex chose tiers on 2026-10-27 (#2796). Each row has a **Tier** cell after its Feature, and a **Role** cell after the Tier (#5290, epic #5288). The tier says which clients must have the feature. The Mac window and the web page are full clients. The tiers trim only the Remote, except Web + Mac link (#4937), which the Mac window does not draw.

- **Core:** all three clients. A change is made on all three in the same branch, or a parity issue is filed for each client that does not get it yet.
- **Mac + web:** the Mac window and the web page. The Remote does not have them: #2798 removes each one from the Remote completely, with no line in its place (Alex, 2026-10-29). Nobody files a Remote parity issue. Until a feature is removed, the Remote's cell says what the Remote has now.
- **Web + Mac link (#4937, epic #4936):** the web page has the feature. The Mac window shows one link to it, in the place where a person looks for it, and draws nothing more. The Remote gets nothing, as for Mac + web. It is for features that people use rarely and that cost much to build natively. Each link shows only when the web page is served and this Mac can open it (the rule of **Edit in Console**, #1002 D17), so a Mac with no web page has no link and no feature; opens the right place (the section, page, scope and range, as `#/reports/…`), not the Overview; and signs the browser in as **Open in Browser** does (with the control plane on this Mac, it pairs the default browser in the same step the first time; with the control plane somewhere else, the web page's own sign-in, which keeps the address). The link carries no code or token. Until the link is there, the row's Mac cell says so and the row is a **delta**.
- **Mac only, Web only and Remote only (#4759; was One client):** one client only, by design. No follow-up, no link and no parity issue. The tier names the client, so a count and a list say which is which:
  - **Mac only:** hover, keyboard, Finder, the menu bar, the Mac's own host and what only a Mac can do.
  - **Web only:** the web page has it and the Mac window has no link to it. It differs from Web + Mac link, where the Mac window shows a link to the page.
  - **Remote only:** none now.

**Core** is:
- the session list, chat, question and permission cards, the prompt bar, approvals, notices and the Brief (the first list in #2796);
- the session and folder work the Remote keeps (#2798): terminals, reading and editing files, diffs and Changes, the Worktrees list, the Simulator pane and the Agent Browser (Alex, 2026-11-07, #3800);
- runtimes' details (accounts, Reaches the Model By, Mark Available), the Auto runtime on the new-session form and in the chat (Alex, 2026-10-29, #2923), personas on the new-session form (Alex, 2026-10-29, #2924), the Events and Resources pages, and MCP app views and their pins;
- the workflow page's Activity, Run Now, Approve and Enabled; today's cost in Activity and the limit banners;
- the join and sign-in approval cards, pairing and sign-in, and the strips that say a machine or the control plane is away;
- the Remote's trimmed Settings: This app (Voice, Notifications, Sidebar) and Help;
- the daily summary: its Off / Summary control and time in Settings ▸ Agent Configuration ▸ Workflows, the summary page and its notice (Alex, 2026-11-01, #3192, #3351).
- day-to-day coding from the Remote (Alex, 2026-11-07, #4030): the person's Secrets and For One Session, Accounts, the Machines group (its rows, New, Start, Stop, Delete, what a machine needs, and a machine's page), a machine's runtimes' **Sign In…** and **Update**, **Add Folder…** and **Clone Git URL…**, and **Move to Machine…**.
- Work ▸ Features: its groups, its features and their epics and issues, with no Board menu (Alex, 2026-11-09).
- the Velocity page, the PRs that landed over time, under Status after the Multiplier on the Remote (Alex, 2026-11-11, #4758).

**Mac + web** is: the Console's pages that the Mac draws in Settings (People and Groups, Audit and Policy); every control plane management page (Sign-ins, People, Groups, Bots and their caps, Channels, Sign-in Providers, Policy and its kill switch, Secrets, Backups, Git access, Add a Browser); Add a Machine, machine providers, Start a Machine from a Repository and Versions; Connectors and connector set-up (Connect your tools, GitLab, Email); the first build's question page and plan page (#5048) and New App… (#5132); Agent Configuration (Skills, Personas and Workflows editors) and Auto's settings (#2923, #2924); the workflow page's Configuration pane, Edit File… and Delete…; folder management (a folder's Project, Archive, Archived folders, Export/Import and the Drop Box); the usage pages (Cost, Tokens, Time, Milestones and their charts; Velocity is Core, #4758); and Folder Settings (Alex, 2026-10-29, #2798). Add Folder, Clone Git URL, the person's Secrets and Accounts, the Machines group and Move to Machine moved to Core (Alex, 2026-11-07, #4030).

**Web + Mac link** is: Reports (every page: Spend, Outcomes, Time, Planning and Delivery, Runtimes, Adoption, Workflows, Features and Saved Views; link #4938); and the Console's Report Policy, Catalogue, Read API and Requests (links #4939) (Alex, 2026-11-12, #4936). The Console's other pages are Mac + web when the Mac draws them in Settings, or draws a part of them with **Edit in Console**, and Web only when the Mac has nothing of them (the list below).

**Web only** is (#4759):
- by #3224 (Alex, 2026-11-01: the Mac window is for the engineer who runs the agents and their own usage; reports and figures about other people are for the web page, where admins work): the Console's admin pages about every machine and person that the Mac has no part of (Machines and its policy column, Projects, Live Now, Audit ▸ Sinks and Team Workflows), Cost ▸ Reports on the web's Cost page, and the Time page's approval wait;
- the web console's own frame (#2141, #2142): the bar of sections, its look and Search or jump to…, the Overview, and Agents' filters and pages; the window stays built around this Mac's folders;
- the web's own ways in: Sign up on the web (#1807) and the page on this Mac with the control plane elsewhere (#1480).

A new report, a figure about other people, or a team or admin feature is Web only (#3224), or Web + Mac link when people look for it on the Mac and Alex chooses a link (#4936).

A new row gets its role when it is added, and its tier from the role (above), or from the lists here when they still say so. When a new feature is on none of the lists, its tier is Mac + web, because the Remote keeps only Core by default (Alex, #2796). An issue that wants it on the Remote says why.

